"Have you ever seen a huge, golden full Moon rising behind the rooftops? A few hours later, high in the sky, it looks small and ordinary. Almost everyone sees this, but the Moon has not shrunk. This is the Moon illusion, one of the oldest puzzles about the sky.": "829fb4dca3e8",
"Have you ever seen a huge, golden full Moon rising behind the rooftops? A few hours later, high in the sky, it looks small and ordinary. Almost everyone sees this, but the Moon has not shrunk. This is the Moon illusion, one of the oldest puzzles about the sky. You can test it yourself. Hold a pea or a pencil eraser at arm's length and close one eye. It just covers the Moon, whether the Moon is near the horizon or high overhead. The Moon always takes up about half a degree of the sky. In fact, the rising Moon is a tiny bit smaller, because it is farther from you: when the Moon is overhead, you are closer to it by about 6,400 kilometers, the distance from Earth's surface to its center. So the illusion happens in our brain, not in the sky. Scientists still debate exactly how it works. One idea is that the brain treats the sky like a flattened dome, so the horizon seems farther away than the point overhead, and a Moon that seems farther away but covers the same angle must be bigger. Another idea is that trees and buildings near the horizon make the Moon appear large by comparison. Whatever the reason, you can fool the trick: look at the rising Moon through a paper tube, or bend over and look at it upside down between your legs, and it suddenly looks smaller. The Moon's true size in the sky does change, just slowly. Its orbit is an oval, so its distance changes from about 356,000 to 407,000 kilometers. A full Moon near its closest point, often called a supermoon, looks about 14 percent wider than one at its farthest, a micromoon. The difference is real but hard to notice, because there is no second Moon in the sky to compare it with. More than 2,000 years ago, a Chinese story in the Liezi told of two children arguing about the Sun. One said the morning Sun looks as big as a carriage cover but the noon Sun only as big as a plate, so it must be closer in the morning. The other said the noon Sun feels as hot as boiling water, so it must be closer at noon. Even Confucius could not decide who was right. About a thousand years ago, the Arab scientist Ibn al-Haytham explained that the Sun and Moon only seem larger near the horizon because of the way we judge distance. What about photos of a giant Moon behind a lighthouse? They are taken from far away with a long telephoto lens, which makes both the Moon and the lighthouse look large. With your own eyes, the Moon is always about the size of a pea at arm's length. Look below for tonight's moonrise over Changhua and this year's biggest and smallest full Moons.": "77f680274316",
